  "sections": [
    {
      "sectionId": "section-introduction",
      "heading": "Introduction",
      "sourceAnchor": "introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Changelings are the closest that players will ever get to playing a\n\tdoppelganger, gifted with the ability to change their appearance without\n\trelying on spells or on items like a disguise kit. While there is more to the\n\trace, this is generally what draws players to the Changeling. Changelings\n\tfirst appeared in Eberron: Rising From the Last War and have been a staple\n\trace in the Eberron setting since 3rd edition."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "In the original version of Eberron: Rising from the Last War, the Changeling\n\tcould apply their flexible +1 ability increase to any ability score, including\n\tCharisma, which allowed them to start at level 1 with 18 Charisma. This has\n\tbeen corrected in\n\t"
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "errata",
              "href": "https://media.wizards.com/2020/dnd/downloads/ERftLW-Errata.pdf"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Shapechanger is versatile, but also limited. Since your clothing and\n\tequipment aren’t covered by the effect, you may need to partially rely on\n\tmundane disguises or spells to pass yourself off as a specific person. It’s\n\tunclear if you need to make checks to deceive other creatures while disguised\n\tin this way, but as a DM I might apply disadvantage on other creatures to\n\trealize that you’re disguised based solely on appearance. It won’t help you\n\ttalk, but the Changeling gets a Charisma increase for that, as well as two\n\tFace skills."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-4",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "For classes which don’t rely solely on Charisma, the Changeling competes for\n\tspace with the Variant Human and with the Half-Elf. Variant humans can get\n\tsimilar Ability Score Increases, and with the Skilled feat can pick up two\n\tskills and proficiency in the Disguise Kit to roughly match the Changeling.\n\tThe Half-elf gets an additional free increase, Darkvision, Fey Ancestry, and\n\tany two skills rather than two from a fixed list, not to mention the\n\tavailability of variants. If you’re playing a changeling, you you want\n\tShapechanger."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-5",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Even with the custom origin rules or the updated version of the Changeling published in Mordenkainen’s Monsters of the Multiverse, the Changeling is still defined by Shapechanger, which is their only trait beyond skill proficiencies which can be matched by numerous other races like the Kenku and the Tabaxi. This predisposes the Changeling to campaigns where infiltration, subterfuge, and intrigue are the norm. Games that are all about crawling dungeons and slaying monsters will be a struggle because the Changeling brings essentially nothing useful in combat."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
