        {
          "blockId": "block-section-disclaimer-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The advice offered below is based on the current "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "State of the 2014 DnD 5e Character Optimization Meta",
              "href": "https://rpgbot.net/dnd5/characters/state-of-the-meta/"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " as of when the article was last updated. Keep in mind that the state of the meta periodically changes as new source materials are released, and the article will be updated accordingly as time allows."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-disclaimer-4",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "We will not include 3rd-party content, Unearthed Arcnaa content, or homebrew in guides for official content because we can’t assume that your game will allow them."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-changeling-versions",
      "heading": "Changeling Versions",
      "sourceAnchor": "changeling-versions",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-changeling-versions-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Changeling was originally introduced to 5e in Eberron: Rising from the\n\tLast War. At the time, their +2 Charisma increase and their +1 flexible\n\tincrease were allowed to stack, allowing them to start with 18 Charisma. This\n\twas changed in errata close to the release of Tasha’s Cauldron of\n\tEverything."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-changeling-versions-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Tasha’s introduced the Customizing Your Origin rules, giving us the second version of the Changeling (let’s call the errata version 1.1). This opened up the Changeling’s skills and let them expand beyond Charisma-based classes, but their predisposition to subterfuge still makes Charisma-based classes among the Changeling’s best options."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-changeling-versions-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Most recently, Mordenkainen’s Monsters of the Multiverse re-published the Changeling. The changes here were minor, replacing the Changelings Ability Score Increase trait with the new standard for ability scores, allowing players to assign either +2/+1 increases or three +1 increases. It also adds Performance to the list of skills available with the Changeling’s two skill proficiencies, allows you to choose to be Medium or Small size, and significantly improves Shapechanger (though it still doesn’t fully replace Disguise Self). Changelings are also fey instead of humanoids now. This update does little to change your best options, unfortunately, so while the Changeling is better at what they’re known for, they’re still only noteworthy in games where infiltration and subterfuge feature heavily."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Changeling Classes (Customizable Origins and MMoM)",
      "sourceAnchor": "classes-custom-origin",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classes-custom-origin-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "This section assumes that you’re using the option “"
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Customizing Your Origin",
              "href": "https://www.dndbeyond.com/sources/tcoe/character-options#CustomizingYourOrigin"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "” rules presented in Tasha’s Cauldron of Everything. If you’re not using those rules, scroll down to the next section."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-artificer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Artificer",
      "sourceAnchor": "artificer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-artificer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Artificers have few capabilities that support the Changeling’s\n\tsubterfuge-heavy capabilities. Perhaps most notable is that the Artificer adds\n\tdouble their proficiency bonus with tools, which includes the Disguise Kit."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-barbarian",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Barbarian",
      "sourceAnchor": "barbarian",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-barbarian-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Barbarians don’t have capabilities that support the Changeling’s\n\tsubterfuge-heavy capabilities."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-bard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Bard",
      "sourceAnchor": "bard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-bard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Likely the Changeling’s best spellcaster option, the combination of abundant\n\tskills, Expertise, and Charisma-based spellcaster synergize well with the\n\tChangeing’s skill options and Shapechanger. College of Whispers feels like a\n\tnatural choice thematically."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-cleric",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Cleric",
      "sourceAnchor": "cleric",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-cleric-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "A trickery cleric might work, and the Changeling’s additional skills help\n\tbring you closer to the Rogue’s capabilities, but you’ll need to balance your\n\tneed for Dexterity and Charisma with the class’s need for Wisdom."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-druid",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Druid",
      "sourceAnchor": "druid",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-druid-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "As useful as turning into an animal is when you need to be sneaky, that\n\tsimply isn’t enough. Druids can’t afford the Charisma to make skills like\n\tDeception work. They typically need decent Dexterity since the Druid’s AC is\n\tterrible, so at least you can manage Stealth."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-fighter",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Fighter",
      "sourceAnchor": "fighter",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-fighter-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Fighters don’t have capabilities that support the Changeling’s\n\tsubterfuge-heavy capabilities."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-monk",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Monk",
      "sourceAnchor": "monk",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-monk-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Monks do well with Stealth, but they can’t afford the Charisma to make\n\tDeception work, so it’s not a great choice for the Changeling."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-paladin",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Paladin",
      "sourceAnchor": "paladin",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-paladin-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Changeling’s best martial option, paladins have enough Charisma to back\n\tup skills like Deception, and a Dexterity-bsed build works fine to support\n\tStealth."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-ranger",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Ranger",
      "sourceAnchor": "ranger",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-ranger-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Rangers do well with Stealth, but they can’t afford the Charisma to make\n\tDeception work, so it’s not a great choice for the Changeling."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-rogue",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Rogue",
      "sourceAnchor": "rogue",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-rogue-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The go-to option for changelings. Abundant skills and expertise work very\n\twell alongside Shapechanger to support any sort of infiltration or other\n\tshenanigans that you want to get into."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-sorcerer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Sorcerer",
      "sourceAnchor": "sorcerer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-sorcerer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "While they don’t have as many skills as the Bard, the Sorcerer is still\n\tCharisma-based, making it easy to use skills like Deception, and with a bit of\n\tDexterity you can easily manage Stealth."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-warlock",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Warlock",
      "sourceAnchor": "warlock",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-warlock-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "While they don’t have as many skills as the Bard, the Warlock is still Charisma-based, making it easy to use skills like Deception, and with a bit of Dexterity you can easily manage Stealth. Mask of Many faces is partially redundant with Shapechanger, so I recommend avoiding it."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-wizard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Wizard",
      "sourceAnchor": "wizard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-wizard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "If you’re going to play a wizard, just cast Disguise Self."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Changeling Classes (Classic Rules)",
      "sourceAnchor": "classes-default",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classes-default-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "This section assumes that you’re "
            },
            {
              "type": "emphasis",
              "text": "not"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " using the option “Customizing Your Origin” rules presented in Tasha’s Cauldron of Everything or the updated version of the race published in Mordenkainen’s Monsters of the Multiverse. If you are using those rules, scroll up to the previous section."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-artificer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Artificer",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-artificer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-artificer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "You can get the crucial Intelligence increase, but nothing else about the\n\tChangeling complements the Artificer’s capabilities."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-barbarian",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Barbarian",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-barbarian",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-barbarian-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "You can get the crucial Strength increase, but nothing else about the\n\tChangeling complements the Barbarian’s capabilities."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-bard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Bard",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-bard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-bard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "18 Charisma at first level, two Face skills, and Shapechanger. Thematically\n\tperfect for College of Whispers, but you can make the changeling work very\n\twell for any bard build."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-cleric",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Cleric",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-cleric",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-cleric-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "You can get the crucial Wisdom increase, and with the Changeling’s free\n\tadditional proficiencies and the Cleric’s class skill list you can easily play\n\ta Face with minimal investment in Charisma."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-druid",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Druid",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-druid",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-druid-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "You can get the crucial Wisdom increase, but the Druid has nearly no use for\n\tCharisma, and if you don’t want to be yourself the Druid typically turns into\n\tan animal rather than turning into another humanoid."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-fighter",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Fighter",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-fighter",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-fighter-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "You can get the crucial Strength/Dexterity increase, and the Charisma\n\tincrease and addition skills work great for a Purple Dragon Knight, but you\n\tcan get that from the Half-Elf or from the Variant Human. Shapechanger has\n\tlittle use for most fighters."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-monk",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Monk",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-monk",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-monk-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "One free increase isn’t enough."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-paladin",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Paladin",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-paladin",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-paladin-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "+1 to either Strength or Dexterity, an increase to Charisma, and additional\n\tskills so that you don’t need to put every single one of your skills into Face\n\tskills. Shapechanger is unusual for paladins, but if you use it more like a\n\tfun character quirk than like a tool for tricking people it could be a lot of\n\trun."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-ranger",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Ranger",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-ranger",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-ranger-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "You can get the crucial Dexterity increase, and with the additional skills\n\tyou can almost keep up with the Rogue."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-rogue",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Rogue",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-rogue",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-rogue-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The obvious options for changelings, the Rogue is better-suited to tricking\n\tpeople and general skulduggery than any other class. If anyone can capitalize\n\ton Shapechanger it’s the Rogue. Two additional skills piles on top of the\n\tRogue’s already spectacular advantage with skills, and Dexterity and Charisma\n\tincreases work great for a Face."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-sorcerer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Sorcerer",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-sorcerer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-sorcerer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "18 Charisma at level 1."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-warlock",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Warlock",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-warlock",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-warlock-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "18 Charisma at level 1. Mask of Many faces is partially-redundant with\n\tShapechanger, so I recommend avoiding it."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-wizard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Wizard",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-wizard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-wizard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "You can get the crucial Intelligence increase, but little else about the\n\tChangeling complements the Wizard’s capabilities. I could see a changeling\n\tsorcerer being fun, but I don’t know if it’s better than a sorcerer with lots\n\tof illusion spells."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
